This paragraph is a light, joking exchange about a basketball team and a football team, with no real moral evaluation. The one candidate is "I abused my power to retain the floor," which is a self-description of procedural misconduct on the House floor. That is a moral-type characterization of an action (abuse of power, i.e., a transgression in an authority role), so it meets the AUTHORITY definition: "misuse of power" and "betrayal of one's role." Because the speaker frames it as an abuse, I code AUTHORITY as present. The sentence is self-deprecating and tongue-in-cheek, but the definition covers implied evaluation, and labeling the action as an abuse of power is an evaluation in those terms.

Because AUTHORITY is present, MORAL RHETORIC is coded 1 under the rule that moral rhetoric is present whenever an action is presented as a transgression.

